.\" Installed by install.sh as /usr/local/share/man/man7/mosaico.7, from its own
.\" directory or downloaded from the repository.
.\"
.\" @SCRIPT_VERSION@ is substituted with the installer's version at install time.
.\"
.\" Every path here duplicates a constant in install.sh (DATA_DIR, SECRETS_DIR,
.\" QUADLET_DIR, ...). Changing one means editing this page too; nothing checks it.
.TH MOSAICO 7 "" "mosaicoctl @SCRIPT_VERSION@" "Mosaico"
.SH NAME
mosaico \- The Data Platform for Robotics and Physical AI
.SH DESCRIPTION
Mosaico is a blazing-fast data platform designed to close the loop between
physical world data and scalable, production-grade systems. Mosaico does one
thing exceptionally well: it transforms monolithic sensor logs into a
structured, queryable archive built for multi-modal data.
.PP
Robotics and Physical AI systems produce sensor data, recorded or live, as
single streams, several concurrent streams, or streams already interleaved
across sensors in formats such as \fB.mcap\fR. mosaicod, the Mosaico daemon,
ingests all of these into dataset frames organized as sequences and topics,
and hands them back through an SDK that can query by sequence, by topic, by
ontology, or by chaining filters together to correlate across sensor types.
Ready-made ingestion pipelines for common robotics tasks, such as
manipulation, are shipped as \fIAlchemies\fR.
.PP
On this host mosaicod is supervised by systemd as \fBmosaicod.service\fR (see
\fBSERVICE MANAGEMENT\fR and \fBQUADLET\fR below for how) and keeps its
metadata in PostgreSQL (see \fBDATABASE\fR below for that). Everything was set
up by \fBmosaicoctl\fR(8).
.PP
This page describes what the installer put on this machine. For the daemon's
concepts, configuration keys and API, see the online documentation at
https://docs.mosaico.dev.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B mosaicod \fIcommand\fB ...
Host wrapper around the daemon CLI, installed at /usr/local/bin/mosaicod. It
forwards its arguments into the running \fBmosaicod\fR service, so it behaves
like a native binary. Run
.B mosaicod --help
for the commands the installed version supports.
.IP
The work needs root, because the service belongs to root's Podman and the
environment file is readable only by root, but you do not have to ask for it:
called from an unprivileged shell the wrapper re-executes itself through
\fBsudo\fR, so \fBmosaicod\fR works as an ordinary command and prompts for a
password if sudo asks for one. To drop that prompt, grant the command
\fINOPASSWD\fR in a \fI/etc/sudoers.d\fR entry. It forwards into the running
service, so it reports the service as missing when the daemon is down.
.TP
.B mosaicoctl \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
A copy of the installation script, kept at /usr/local/bin/mosaicoctl. Use
it to re-run the installation, change the configuration, upgrade to another image
tag or uninstall. Run
.B mosaicoctl --help
for the full list of options.
.SH SERVICE MANAGEMENT
\fBmosaicod.service\fR is an ordinary systemd service, managed with the usual
tools:
.RS 4
.nf
systemctl status mosaicod.service
systemctl restart mosaicod.service
journalctl -u mosaicod.service -f
.fi
.RE
.PP
The same applies to \fBmosaico-postgres.service\fR when the database is
embedded. The systemd journal covers what mosaicod itself logs; for the raw
output of the underlying Podman container, see \fBQUADLET\fR below.
.PP
The daemon serves on the port recorded as \fBMOSAICOD_PORT\fR in
\fI/var/lib/mosaico/install.conf\fR. That is 6726, the port clients and the SDK
assume, unless the installation was given \fB--port\fR. That port is published
on the address recorded as \fBMOSAICOD_HOST\fR in the same file: 127.0.0.1,
which is this machine only, unless the installation was given \fB--host\fR.
\fB--host 0.0.0.0\fR publishes it on every address of the host instead, which is
what makes Mosaico reachable from the network.
.SH DATABASE
mosaicod keeps its metadata in PostgreSQL, reached through the
\fBMOSAICOD_DB_URL\fR connection string in
\fI/var/lib/mosaico/secrets/mosaicod.env\fR. Which of the two arrangements below
is in use was recorded as \fBPG_MODE\fR in \fI/var/lib/mosaico/install.conf\fR.
.TP
.B embedded
The default. PostgreSQL runs as \fBmosaico-postgres.service\fR and is not
published to the host: only mosaicod reaches it. The password is generated on
the first run, kept in \fI/var/lib/mosaico/secrets/postgres_password\fR, reused
by every later run and never printed. The data lives in the
\fBmosaico-postgres-data\fR Podman volume.
.TP
.B external
A PostgreSQL instance you already run, given to the installer with
\fB--pg-host\fR and friends. No database service is created and none is
supervised: the installer only writes the connection string. The database and the
role have to exist before the installation, and the role needs enough privilege
to create the schema mosaicod uses on first start. Backups, upgrades and
availability of that instance stay yours.
.PP
An external instance running on this same host cannot be reached at
\fI127.0.0.1\fR from mosaicod.service: that address is its own loopback, so
the installer rewrites a loopback host to \fIhost.containers.internal\fR, which
Podman resolves to the host. For that to work PostgreSQL must listen on an
address other than the host loopback (\fBlisten_addresses\fR in
\fIpostgresql.conf\fR) and \fIpg_hba.conf\fR must accept connections from
mosaicod's network. Passing the host's LAN address as \fB--pg-host\fR instead
avoids the indirection altogether.
.PP
Re-running the installer with \fB--pg-host\fR against an installation that had
an embedded database stops \fBmosaico-postgres.service\fR and removes its
unit. The \fBmosaico-postgres-data\fR volume is deliberately left in place, so
nothing is lost and switching back reuses the old database.
.SH TLS
mosaicod does not issue certificates. Whatever their origin, the certificate
chain and the private key are copied to
\fI/var/lib/mosaico/secrets/tls/cert.pem\fR and
\fI/var/lib/mosaico/secrets/tls/key.pem\fR, mounted read-only into mosaicod.service,
and read by the daemon only at startup.
.PP
When the installer obtained the certificate from Let's Encrypt, renewal is
automatic. The certbot timer shipped by the distribution performs the renewal, and
\fI/etc/letsencrypt/renewal-hooks/deploy/mosaico.sh\fR copies each renewed
certificate into place and restarts the service. Check that both halves are in
order with:
.RS 4
.nf
systemctl list-timers certbot.timer
sudo certbot renew --dry-run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Renewal repeats the same challenge as the first issuance, so \fBport 443 has to
stay open for the TLS challenge\fR. A firewall rule added after installation is
the usual reason a renewal that used to work stops working.
.PP
For a certificate supplied with \fB--tls-cert\fR and \fB--tls-key\fR no hook is
installed, and renewal is yours to perform: overwrite the two files and restart
the service.
.RS 4
.nf
sudo install -m 600 fullchain.pem /var/lib/mosaico/secrets/tls/cert.pem
sudo install -m 600 privkey.pem /var/lib/mosaico/secrets/tls/key.pem
sudo systemctl restart mosaicod.service
.fi
.RE
.SH API KEYS
When the installation requested a master API key, the daemon runs with key
enforcement on and every client must present one. The master key is stored in
\fI/var/lib/mosaico/secrets/master_api_key\fR and holds \fBread|write|delete\fR,
which since mosaicod 0.6 is the whole set: the \fBmanage\fR permission of earlier
releases was removed in favour of granular ones, so a key that grants all three
is the closest equivalent.
.PP
Managing keys is not a permission. The commands below reach the database
directly, so what they need is the ability to run them in the container: no key
is presented, and one of these commands works even when enforcement is off:
.RS 4
.nf
mosaicod api-key create --permissions read
mosaicod api-key list
mosaicod api-key revoke \fIfingerprint\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
Without that request the daemon accepts unauthenticated clients on every
address the port was published on.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/mosaico/install.conf
Non-secret record of the choices made at install time, including the port the
daemon serves on and the address it is published on, the storage backend and
the storage directory in use, the database mode and, for an external database,
its host, port, database name and role.
.TP
.I /var/lib/mosaico/secrets
Generated secrets, readable by root only: the daemon environment file, the TLS
material under \fItls/\fR, the PostgreSQL password and \fIpostgres.env\fR when
the database is embedded and, when requested, the master API key.
.TP
.I /opt/mosaico
Present only when the installer was run with \fB--sdk\fR: a Python virtual
environment holding the \fBmosaicolabs\fR SDK. Activate it with
\fB. /opt/mosaico/bin/activate\fR. Removed by \fB--uninstall\fR.
.TP
.I /etc/letsencrypt/renewal-hooks/deploy/mosaico.sh
Present only when the certificate came from Let's Encrypt. Run by certbot after
a renewal, it refreshes the copies under \fI/var/lib/mosaico/secrets/tls\fR and
restarts the daemon. Removed by \fB--uninstall\fR; the certificate itself is left
in \fI/etc/letsencrypt\fR, since other services on the host may be using it.
.TP
.I ~/.mosaico/ai-manifest
Present only when the installer was run with \fB--ai\fR, in the home of the user
who ran it: the list of skill files written for that user's AI assistants. See
\fBAI ASSISTANTS\fR below.
.PP
Dataset frames live either in the local directory recorded as \fBFS_DIR\fR in
\fIinstall.conf\fR, or in the configured object store bucket.
.PP
The unit files behind \fBmosaicod.service\fR and \fBmosaico-postgres.service\fR
are covered separately in \fBQUADLET\fR below.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is passed to the daemon through
\fI/var/lib/mosaico/secrets/mosaicod.env\fR, written by the installer. Re-running
\fBmosaicoctl\fR is the supported way to change it; the generated
PostgreSQL password and master API key are preserved across runs. Hand-made
edits to that file are preserved too, but only until the next run overwrites
them.
.PP
Only the options given to the installer are written to that file, so any
setting left out keeps the daemon's own default.
.PP
Retention is not configured there. The routine is a separate command, so its two
values are flags on \fImosaico-cleanup.container\fR: \fB--time-interval\fR and
\fB--retention-duration\fR, both in seconds. Re-run the installer with
\fB--cleanup-time-interval\fR and \fB--cleanup-retention\fR to change them, or
with \fB--no-cleanup\fR to remove the container altogether. The values are
documented at https://docs.mosaico.dev/config/cleanup.
.PP
The installer pins the image tag rather than letting it track \fIlatest\fR,
because the generated command line and environment variables belong to one
mosaicod release. Upgrade by re-running the installer with
\fB--version\fR, not by pulling a newer image under the same unit.
.SH AI ASSISTANTS
When the installer was run with \fB--ai\fR, it also installed Mosaico's skills
for AI coding assistants, for the user who ran it through \fBsudo\fR: into that
user's home, never into root's. Only the download runs as root; the archive
is unpacked and checked, and the files written and removed, by that user,
through \fBrunuser\fR(1), \fBsudo\fR or \fBsu\fR, so they are owned by
that user. They come from the \fI.agents/skills/\fR folder of
\%https://github.com/mosaico-labs/mosaico, at the one commit the installer
pins by its full sha, downloaded over HTTPS alone (a redirect to plain HTTP is
refused) and checked against the SHA-256 of their content that the installer
carries: when the two differ, or when the folder holds anything but
directories and regular files, nothing is installed. Each
skill folder is copied as it is into both of the folders below, whichever
assistants are installed. Nothing else in \fI.agents/\fR is installed. Until
the skills are released no commit is pinned, and \fB--ai\fR says so and
installs nothing, the rest of the installation going on.
.TP
.I ~/.claude/skills/<name>/
Read by Claude Code. \%https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills
.TP
.I ~/.agents/skills/<name>/
The shared user-level skills folder, read by:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Codex: \%https://developers.openai.com/codex/skills
.IP \(bu 2
GitHub Copilot CLI:
\%https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/how-tos/copilot-cli/customize-copilot/create-skills
.IP \(bu 2
Cursor: \%https://cursor.com/docs/skills
.IP \(bu 2
Gemini CLI: \%https://geminicli.com/docs/cli/skills/
.RE
.PP
Antigravity CLI uses a different folder and is not covered.
.PP
Every file written is listed, with its SHA-256, in \fI~/.mosaico/ai-manifest\fR.
Re-running the installer with \fB--ai\fR updates a listed file only while its
content is still the one recorded, so a file you have edited is kept, with a
warning, and a file the installer did not write is never touched. A listed file
that Mosaico no longer ships, because a skill was removed or renamed, is deleted
on the same condition; an edited one is kept and dropped from the manifest. To
delete the
listed files that are still as installed, and then the manifest (a listed path
outside the two folders above is refused and left in place):
.RS 4
.nf
mosaicoctl --ai --remove
.fi
.RE
.PP
The skills are written for Mosaico 0.8 or newer, compared on major and minor
version alone. When the installation is for an older version, the default one
included, Mosaico is installed as usual and the skills are skipped with a
warning: add them once you run 0.8 or newer. A version that is not a release
number, such as \fIlatest\fR or a commit (a short sha made of digits alone
included), cannot be compared and is accepted with a warning. \fB--version\fR
is an option that installs Mosaico, so this installs Mosaico 0.8.0 and the
skills together:
.RS 4
.nf
sudo mosaicoctl --ai --version 0.8.0
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fB-y\fR is an option that installs Mosaico as well: \fBsudo mosaicoctl -y
--ai\fR installs Mosaico with its defaults and the skills, which are skipped
with a warning while the default version is older than 0.8.
.PP
Given with no option that installs Mosaico (\fB--dry-run\fR aside),
\fB--ai\fR installs the skills and nothing else: no daemon, container
or service is installed or changed, and nothing is asked. To add only the
skills, or to list the files that would be written without writing any:
.RS 4
.nf
mosaicoctl --ai
mosaicoctl --ai --dry-run
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fB--ai\fR needs no \fBsudo\fR: the files go to the home of the user who
runs it. On a machine where \fBmosaicoctl\fR is not installed:
.RS 4
.nf
curl -fsSL https://get.mosaico.dev | sh -s -- --ai
.fi
.RE
.PP
The version is then
checked only when the environment variable \fBMOSAICO_VERSION\fR is set, since
the skills may be meant for a Mosaico running on another machine, and an older
one is refused; without it, a note says that the skills are written for
Mosaico 0.8 or newer (the main branch). \fBMOSAICO_VERSION\fR does not by
itself install Mosaico. \fBsudo\fR drops it from the caller's environment, so
under \fBsudo\fR give it after \fBsudo\fR; without \fBsudo\fR:
.RS 4
.nf
MOSAICO_VERSION=0.8.0 mosaicoctl --ai
.fi
.RE
.PP
A download, an unpack or a check that
fails then makes it exit with status 1; skills not released yet, or a run as
root with no \fBSUDO_USER\fR, end with 0 and a note.
.PP
\fB--dry-run\fR together with \fB--ai\fR lists the two folders and every file
that would be written, and writes none of them. \fB--ai\fR cannot be combined
with \fB--uninstall\fR, which leaves the skills in place.
.PP
For testing, the environment variable \fBMOSAICO_AI_SOURCE\fR names a local
folder holding a \fIskills/\fR folder to install from instead of the download;
its content is not checked against the pinned SHA-256, and a warning says so.
.SH UNINSTALLING
To stop and remove the services, their Quadlet units (see \fBQUADLET\fR below),
the two commands and this man page, leaving all stored data in place:
.RS 4
.nf
sudo mosaicoctl --uninstall
.fi
.RE
.PP
Stored data is never touched by that command. To wipe it as well, remove the
secrets, the storage directory recorded as \fBFS_DIR\fR in \fIinstall.conf\fR
and, for an embedded database, the volume it lives in:
.RS 4
.nf
sudo rm -rf /var/lib/mosaico/secrets /var/lib/mosaico/install.conf
sudo rm -rf \fI<storage directory>\fR
sudo podman volume rm mosaico-postgres-data
.fi
.RE
.PP
The exact commands, with the storage directory filled in, are printed by
\fBmosaicoctl --uninstall\fR when it finishes. Data kept in an external
object store, or in an external PostgreSQL instance, must be removed there.
.PP
Skills installed with \fB--ai\fR are left in place as well, since
they are of use without a local daemon. Remove them first, while
\fBmosaicoctl\fR is still there, with \fBmosaicoctl --ai --remove\fR (see
\fBAI ASSISTANTS\fR).
.SH QUADLET
\fBmosaicod.service\fR and \fBmosaico-postgres.service\fR are not hand-written
systemd units: systemd generates them at boot, through Podman Quadlet, from the
unit files below.
.TP
.I /etc/containers/systemd/mosaicod.container
Defines the Podman container backing \fBmosaicod.service\fR: image, port
publication, mounts for the secrets and TLS material, and the environment file.
.TP
.I /etc/containers/systemd/mosaico.network
Defines \fBmosaico.network\fR, the internal Podman network mosaicod and, when
the database is embedded, mosaico-postgres share. It is not published to the
host.
.TP
.I /etc/containers/systemd/mosaico-postgres.container
Present only for an embedded database. Defines the Podman container backing
\fBmosaico-postgres.service\fR, including the \fBmosaico-postgres-data\fR
volume mount.
.PP
Hand edits to these files take effect only after \fBsystemctl daemon-reload\fR,
and are undone the next time \fBmosaicoctl\fR runs, since it regenerates
them from scratch; re-running the installer is the supported way to change what
they contain.
.PP
Quadlet names each container after the service it backs, minus the
\fB.service\fR suffix, so Podman's own view of it (image, mounts, raw
stdout/stderr) is reachable directly:
.RS 4
.nf
podman inspect mosaicod
podman logs mosaicod
podman logs mosaico-postgres
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR podman (1),
.BR podman-systemd.unit (5)
.PP
